# Kubernetes & Helm Testing Rules

## `helm lint`

Runs before every commit. Zero warnings allowed. Zero errors allowed.

```bash
helm lint ./charts/<chart-name>
helm lint ./charts/<chart-name> -f values-staging.yaml
helm lint ./charts/<chart-name> -f values-prod.yaml
```

Lint all values file combinations in CI. A chart that lints with defaults but fails with staging values is broken.

## `helm template`

Render the chart locally to inspect the full YAML output before pushing. Catches Go template syntax errors and missing value references before CI.

```bash
helm template my-release ./charts/<chart-name> \
  -f values.yaml \
  -f values-staging.yaml \
  --debug
```

Pipe the output to a file and inspect it. If the rendered output looks wrong, the chart is wrong — not Kubernetes.

## `kubeconform`

Validate rendered manifests against Kubernetes API schemas. Run in CI on every PR.

```bash
helm template my-release ./charts/<chart-name> \
  -f values.yaml \
  -f values-staging.yaml \
  | kubeconform -strict -summary
```

Use `-strict` mode to catch unknown fields. Use `-kubernetes-version` to match the target cluster version. A manifest that doesn't match the API schema will fail to apply.

## `helm unittest`

Unit test chart template logic with the helm-unittest plugin:

```bash
helm plugin install https://github.com/helm-unittest/helm-unittest
helm unittest ./charts/<chart-name>
```

Write unit tests for any non-trivial template logic:
- Conditional blocks (`{{- if .Values.ingress.enabled }}`)
- Value overrides that change resource names or labels
- Optional sidecar containers or init containers
- Resource limit calculations

Place test files in `charts/<chart-name>/tests/` with `_test.yaml` suffix.

## `kind` or `k3d` for Integration Tests

Spin up a local cluster in CI for PRs that touch chart templates:

```bash
# Create cluster
kind create cluster --name ci-test

# Install chart
helm install my-release ./charts/<chart-name> \
  -f values.yaml \
  --wait --timeout 120s

# Run smoke tests (e.g., check pod is running, service responds)
kubectl get pods -l app.kubernetes.io/name=<chart-name>
kubectl wait --for=condition=ready pod -l app.kubernetes.io/name=<chart-name> --timeout=60s

# Cleanup
kind delete cluster --name ci-test
```

Integration tests are required for PRs touching Deployment templates, Service templates, or Ingress templates. Skip for changes to values files only (use helm unittest instead).

## checkov for Security Scanning

Run `checkov` in CI to catch missing security contexts, privileged containers, and missing resource limits:

```bash
# Install: pip install checkov
checkov -d ./charts/<chart-name> --framework helm

# Or against rendered templates:
helm template my-release ./charts/<chart-name> | checkov -f - --framework kubernetes
```

All HIGH severity findings must be resolved before merge. MEDIUM findings require a documented justification in the PR if not fixed. LOW findings are advisory.

Common checkov catches:
- Missing `securityContext.runAsNonRoot`
- Missing `securityContext.allowPrivilegeEscalation: false`
- Missing resource limits
- Containers running as root
- Missing read-only root filesystem
